  <h1>PR and press for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Press is one of the most powerful things a firm can own and one of the worst
    things to build a growth plan on. The whole game is knowing which job it actually
    does, and it is not the job most firms chase it for.
  </p>

  <p>
    The biggest architecture sites pull a serious crowd. <a href="https://www.semrush.com/trending-websites/global/architecture" rel="nofollow">Semrush estimates</a> archdaily.com at about 7.3 million visits and dezeen.com at 6.8 million in September 2026. ArchDaily <a href="https://www.archdaily.com/content/about" rel="nofollow">describes itself</a> as a resource for architects, designers and professionals, and that is who shows up. It is a magnificent trophy handed out at a party your clients were never invited to.
  </p>
  <p>
    So press is not useless. It is misfiled. Firms reach for it as a way to get found,
    which it is terrible at, when its real value is something else entirely: a way to be
    believed once someone has already found you. Get that backwards and you spend a year
    chasing bylines and wonder why the phone stays quiet.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Press and awards are a proof layer, not a lead engine. Design-press readers are mostly your peers, and when we asked four AI engines how to choose an architect, none cited a design magazine and none told the buyer to look for press coverage. Aim residential stories at local and consumer outlets, commercial ones at business press, use the coverage as proof your ads and proposals point at, collect the backlink, and let the engine that creates demand do the finding.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This page is about where press and awards actually sit in the wider system of <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a>, and, more usefully, where they do not. Because it is a genuinely good asset, held wrong-end-first by nearly everyone who reaches for it.
  </p>

  <h2>The one distinction that decides everything</h2>
  <p>
    The press that impresses your peers and the press your buyer reads are two different
    mastheads. Winning the first feels far better and does far less. Firms spend years
    confusing them, then frame the award and go back to wondering where the work is.
  </p>
  <p>
    ArchDaily, Dezeen, the design annuals, the AIA honors, they are all real credentials
    and worth having as proof of your craft. But the homeowner planning a renovation and
    the developer picking a firm for a forty-unit build have probably never opened them. That
    audience is architects and students admiring each other's sections. Lovely company.
    None of it is a client.
  </p>
  <p>
    A trade feature is a peer signal and a portfolio line. Treat it as exactly that and
    it earns its place. Treat it as a marketing plan and you have built your growth on
    the approval of the only people in the market who are never going to pay you.
  </p>

  <h2>What press actually buys you</h2>
  <p>
    One thing, and it is worth having: credibility you did not write yourself. Just be honest about its size. Nielsen's <a href="https://www.nielsen.com/insights/2013/under-the-influence-consumer-trust-in-advertising/" rel="nofollow">2013 trust survey</a> (more than 29,000 people in 58 countries, fielded February and March 2013) found 67 percent trusted editorial content such as newspaper articles. Branded websites scored 69 percent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read that twice. A news story about you and your own website sit in the same band. The only thing clearly above both is a recommendation from someone the buyer knows, 84 percent in 2013 and still top of the list in Nielsen's <a href="https://www.nielsen.com/insights/2021/beyond-martech-building-trust-with-consumers-and-engaging-where-sentiment-is-high/" rel="nofollow">2021 edition</a> (more than 40,000 people, surveyed September 2021), where 88 percent trusted it more than any other channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    (An earlier version of this page said a news article is trusted far more than anything a firm says about itself. Nielsen's own table says otherwise, so we fixed it.)
  </p>
  <p>
    So the edge is practical rather than magical. An article is a second, independent voice saying what your site already says, and a buyer who is weighing you up can find it, check it, and see that someone with nothing to sell agreed. It makes your <a href="/website-design-for-architects">website</a> more convincing, your proposals heavier, and every other thing you run land harder. It closes the gap between interested and convinced. What it does not do is open that gap in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>What AI tells your buyer about press and awards</h2>
  <p>
    In <a href="https://www.brightlocal.com/research/local-consumer-review-survey/" rel="nofollow">BrightLocal's 2026 survey of 1,002 US adults</a>, 45 percent had used ChatGPT or another AI tool for local business recommendations, up from 6 percent a year earlier. So on 10 September 2026 we put four buyer questions to ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini and Claude with web search on, 16 answers in all, and logged every source they cited. ChatGPT returned no citations, so the source counts cover the other three engines.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Buyer question</th><th>Sources cited</th><th>Design press</th><th>Other magazines</th><th>AIA chapters and BSA</th><th>Answers that said check awards</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Choosing an architect for a custom home</td><td>55</td><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0 of 4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Architect cost for a renovation or addition</td><td>82</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0 of 4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Choosing a firm for a commercial building</td><td>63</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>2 of 4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What a developer should look for in an architect</td><td>75</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>22</td><td>0 of 4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>All four questions</td><td>275</td><td>0</td><td>5</td><td>25</td><td>2 of 16</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Zero citations from ArchDaily, Dezeen or any design title, and zero from business press. Not one of the 16 answers told the buyer to look for press coverage. Awards came up twice, both times on the commercial question, as one line in a checklist ("look into any awards or recognitions they have received").
  </p>
  <p>
    The five magazine citations are the interesting part. All five are advice articles answering the buyer's actual question (Colorado Homes' "5 things to consider when choosing an architect", Bob Vila on what an architect costs), and none is a project feature or a profile of a firm. The magazine got cited for being useful to the buyer, which is a different job from praising your work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hold it loosely: four questions, one day, and AI answers move. But the direction is plain.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do residential, the outlets that reach your buyer through AI are consumer home titles, and the way in is a quote or a guide on the question a homeowner asks. If you do commercial, the bodies that showed up were AIA chapters and the Boston Society for Architecture, with their "selecting an architect" pages, so a byline or a listing there is worth more than a design annual. The full pull is in <a href="/ai-search-for-architects">AI search for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The two buyers read two completely different mastheads</h2>
  <p>
    If press is going to reach a buyer at all rather than just your peers, you have to
    aim it, and the two halves of the market read nothing in common. Pick the buyer
    before you pick the outlet.
  </p>

  <h3>If you do residential work</h3>
  <p>
    The homeowner who will hire you is not on ArchDaily. They are reading local. The city
    magazine's list of favorite architects, the regional paper's home and design section,
    a before-and-after feature in a shelter publication, the neighborhood glossy that runs
    a piece on a renovation two streets over.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the press worth chasing here, because it is the press that actually reaches the
    person about to start a project. Reaching them where they already look is the whole job in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a>, and a warm local story does it in a way no peer award can.
  </p>

  <h3>If you do commercial work</h3>
  <p>
    Forget the design annuals entirely. Your buyer is a developer, a business owner, or a
    facilities director, and they read business press, not architecture press. The regional
    business journal, the trade publication of their own industry rather than yours, a
    bylined piece where you sound like someone who understands their timeline and their
    budget rather than their taste.
  </p>
  <p>
    A short, considered placement in the right business outlet does real work over a long
    sales cycle, because it gives a cautious buyer something credible to find while they
    quietly decide. The full commercial picture is in <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>, and press is the third-party proof layer inside it.
  </p>

  <h2>How to earn it without a six-thousand-a-month publicist</h2>
  <p>
    Most firms assume press means hiring an agency and hoping. You can earn a surprising
    amount of it yourself if you stop pitching yourself and start being useful to the
    people who write the stories.
  </p>
  <p>
    Journalists say so out loud. In Muck Rack's <a href="https://media.muckrack.com/documents/State_of_Journalism_2026_1.pdf" rel="nofollow">State of Journalism 2026</a> (897 usable responses, mostly US, surveyed 30 January to 2 March 2026), 88 percent said they delete a pitch that misses their beat and 71 percent delete one that reads as promotional. 43 percent said relevant pitches seldom arrive at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the other side, 40 percent want original data or research with a pitch, and 78 percent said a pitch feels relevant when it directly affects the community their audience belongs to.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Be a source, not a press release. Reporters need expert quotes on deadline constantly. HARO, the free tool everyone used for this, was shut down by Cision in December 2024 and relaunched free by Featured.com in April 2025, and live tools like <a href="https://www.qwoted.com/" rel="nofollow">Qwoted</a> and <a href="https://featured.com/" rel="nofollow">Featured</a> do the same job: they connect you to journalists actively looking for someone who knows your subject. Answer the ones in your lane and get quoted.</li>
    <li>Bring something only you have. "Local firm completes house" is a story no one runs. A real opinion, a local angle, or a number nobody else has ("why the new zoning rewrite will change what your street looks like") is a story an editor actually wants, and it is exactly the community-impact test those journalists described.</li>
    <li>Use awards as proof, not as a plan. Enter the ones your buyer would recognize or that make a strong line in a proposal, then put them on your site. The trophy is a credential you show to the pipeline you built elsewhere.</li>
    <li>Keep it small and human. A short list of local, sector, and trade journalists you actually know beats a blast to five hundred inboxes, and half the journalists in that survey said a mass email gets deleted on sight. Press is a relationship, run at the scale of relationships.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The quiet bonus that outlasts the article</h2>
  <p>
    There is a second payoff most firms miss. When a real publication writes about you, it
    usually links to you, and a link and a brand mention from a trusted outlet are among the
    strongest off-page signals you can earn in search. The clipping ages; the link keeps
    working for years.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is where press and search quietly overlap, and it is why a coverage push doubles as a ranking push. How that compounding layer works, and why it pays you back long after publication, is the whole of <a href="/seo-for-architects#3-authority-which-mostly-means-other-people-vouching-for-you">SEO for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where press stops</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the ceiling, and it is the same one every proof channel has. Press cannot be
    scheduled or scaled. You cannot decide on a Tuesday that you want thirty qualified
    inquiries and produce a magazine feature by Friday, and even if you could, most of the
    people reading it are the wrong people. It builds trust. It does not create demand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which is exactly why it belongs on top of the thing that does create demand, rather than in place of it. The channel that puts your work in front of the actual buyer, on a schedule you control, for both residential and commercial firms, is paid social, and the case for it is in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>. That is the engine. Press is the proof the engine gets to point at.
  </p>
  <p>
    Picture the two together. Your ads put your work in front of the right buyer and capture
    the ones ready to raise a hand. The interested-but-cautious ones go and Google you before
    they book, and there they find the feature, the award, the coverage, and the story flips
    from "some firm ran an ad" to "the firm the local paper wrote about."
  </p>
  <p>
    (The first thing that brand search shows them is your profile, covered in <a href="/google-business-profile-for-architects">Google Business Profile for architects</a>.) How the full pipeline connects is in <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>Should I hire a PR agency?</h3>
  <p>
    A good one that earns you real coverage in outlets your buyer actually reads is worth it,
    as a proof layer. Just do not sign a retainer expecting a pipeline out of it. Generating
    inquiries on a schedule is the engine's job, not the publicist's, and confusing the two is
    how firms burn a year and a budget.
  </p>
  <h3>Is an ArchDaily feature worth chasing?</h3>
  <p>
    As a credential and a peer signal, yes, it is a real thing to be proud of and good proof to
    display. As a way to reach clients, no, because clients are not the audience. Use it as
    evidence on your site, not as your growth plan.
  </p>
  <h3>Are architecture awards worth it?</h3>
  <p>
    Same answer. Excellent proof, a nice wall, and a strong line in a proposal. Enter the ones
    a buyer would recognize, put them where prospects can see them, and do not mistake a trophy
    for a booked project.
  </p>
  <h3>Does press coverage help my firm show up in ChatGPT?</h3>
  <p>
    Less than most firms hope, going by our September 2026 pull. Across 275 sources four AI engines cited on four buyer questions, none came from a design magazine, and only two of 16 answers mentioned awards at all. The magazine pages that did get cited were advice articles answering the buyer's question.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the press that helps with AI is a quote or a guide inside an article on the question your buyer asks, on a consumer home title if you do residential or a professional body's page if you do commercial. A project feature mostly helps a buyer who already found you.
  </p>
  <h3>Isn't all press good press?</h3>
  <p>
    Coverage your buyer never sees mostly flatters your ego and your standing among peers.
    Coverage your buyer can find when they check you out helps you get hired. Both feel like
    press. Only one of them shows up in your calendar, so aim for the second.
  </p>

  <h2>Use press as proof, not as a plan</h2>
  <p>
    Press is borrowed credibility, and aimed right it is genuinely valuable. Aimed at your
    peers it is a lovely photo for the office wall. Aimed as proof behind an engine that
    actually reaches the people who hire you, it turns interested strangers into buyers who
    already half-trust you before the first call.
  </p>
  <p>
    Building that engine, and giving your proof somewhere useful to sit, is the part we run for
    architecture firms. If you would rather have the machine underneath handled while you go
    collect the coverage that makes it convert, that is what we do.
  </p>
